{"id":"1712fd80-0d23-4f1f-b449-42e5bf037ebd","object":"chat.completion","created":1795147637,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"SELECT ca.campaign_id, COUNT(*) AS usage_count\nFROM campaigns ca\nJOIN coupons co ON co.campaign_id = ca.campaign_id\nJOIN coupon_usages cu ON cu.coupon_id = co.coupon_id\nGROUP BY ca.campaign_id\nORDER BY ca.campaign_id\nLIMIT 1000;","reasoning_content":"Need SQL: campaigns join coupons join coupon_usages, count usages per campaign_id, only those with usage records, output campaign_id and count.\n\nCount usage records: coupon_usages primary key usage_id, count(*) per campaign. Could a usage row duplicated? Join coupons via coupon_id unique, so count(*) fine.\n\nGROUP BY campaign_id. Order? not specified; can order by count desc maybe. Just output.\n\nSELECT c.campaign_id, COUNT(*) AS usage_count FROM campaigns c JOIN coupons co ON co.campaign_id=c.campaign_id JOIN coupon_usages cu ON cu.coupon_id=co.coupon_id GROUP BY c.campaign_id ORDER BY c.campaign_id LIMIT? Requirement LIMIT required unless single row aggregate. Use LIMIT 100? Hmm default TopN? No explicit N. Rule 3 must LIMIT. Use LIMIT 100.\n\nActually campaigns table only needed for campaign_id exists in coupons; could query coupons directly. But entity activity id; coupons.campaign_id. Use campaigns join to ensure entity. Fine."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2874,"completion_tokens":288,"total_tokens":3162,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2688},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":217},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2688,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":186},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}